      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Proton (H+) secretion and the HVCN1 H+ channel are part of the innate host defense mechanism of the airways. The objective of this study was to determine H+ secretion in asthmatic and nonasthmatic patients with chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS) in freshly excised human sinonasal tissue."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Nasal or sinus mucosa from subjects with three different conditions (normal, CRS, and CRS with asthma) was harvested during sinus surgery. The equilibrium pH and the rate of H+ secretion were measured in an Ussing chamber using the pH-stat titration technique."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Nasal epithelia isolated from subjects with CRS and asthma had a mucosal equilibrium pH = 6.95 (n = 5), which was significantly lower than in normal subjects (7.35 +/- 0.21; n = 5) or from subjects with CRS without asthma (7.33 +/- 0.15 In = 5). Nasal epithelia from CRS with asthma (n = 5) secreted H+ at a rate of 135 +/- 46 nmol x min(-1) x cm(-2). This rate was significantly higher compared with normal (73 +/- 39 nmol x min(-1) x cm(-2); n = 8) or CRS without asthma (51 +/- 28 nmol x min(-1) x cm(-2); n = 7). Mucosal addition of the HVCN1 blocker ZnCl2 blocked H+ secretion by 70% in normal, 53% in CRS without asthma, and by 51% in CRS with asthma. In contrast, measures in sinus tissues were unaffected by the disease condition."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Freshly excised human nasal and sinus epithelia secrete acid. Nasal (but not sinus) tissues from asthmatic CRS patients showed lower mucosal pH values and higher rates of H+ secretion than CRS and normal subjects. The increased acid secretion might contribute to epithelial injury in CRS patients with asthma."
        }
      ]},
